From: PW3 Android port
Subject: [PW3] Wi-Fi: the idle timer counts awake time and yields to the screen

Patch 0002 made the "screen off for a minute" alarm a non-waking one (RTC, not
RTC_WAKEUP) so that a sleeping system is not woken only to switch off a card
that lost power with the suspend. What that alarm then does was not measured,
and it does two wrong things, both seen on 2026-11-04 (build i, `dumpsys wifi`
records and the device log):

- While the system is awake with the screen off (a cable in: 11:05-11:21), the
  alarm manager holds a non-waking alarm back, up to fifteen minutes when the
  device has been non-interactive that long (AlarmManagerService's
  non-wakeup delay). The minute became sixteen: Wi-Fi switched off at 11:21:22,
  not 11:06:13.
- Whatever is still pending is delivered the moment the device becomes
  interactive, which is the moment the screen comes on. 11:43:37.783:
  CMD_DEVICE_IDLE, DeviceActiveState -> NoLockHeldState, DISABLED_MODE sent;
  11:43:37.786: CMD_SCREEN_ON, back to DeviceActiveState, CONNECT_MODE sent.
  WifiStateMachine was in SupplicantStoppingState for the second message and
  took only its mode from it; it ended in InitialState with nobody to start
  the supplicant again, while WifiController sat in StaEnabledState and so
  ignored every "on" from Settings (`processed=StaEnabledState` for
  CMD_WIFI_TOGGLED at 11:52:20 and 11:52:38; the switch showed on, the page
  reopened off). `svc wifi disable` then `enable` was the only way out. The
  "switched on but never comes up with the screen lit" report of 2026-11-02
  (known-issues, fifteen, kind two) is this: the module unload that failed
  there was a DISABLED_MODE arriving as the device woke.

The timer is now the state machine's own delayed message, on the uptime clock:
sixty seconds of the system being awake with the screen off. Across a suspend
it does not run (the card is unpowered then anyway) and resumes counting on
wake, with the screen still off; the screen coming on removes it. Nothing is
scheduled with the alarm manager any more, so nothing can be held back or
delivered late. Scheduling replaces, as setting the same alarm did: a timer
already pending is removed before the new one is queued (the battery path
re-arms it when a plug comes out with the screen off). And CMD_DEVICE_IDLE is
taken only while the device is not interactive: a message due in the same
instant the screen comes on is stale, and the screen-on that follows would
otherwise cross with it as above. DeviceActiveState asks once per message and
does the whole of the work for it, so that the flag, the battery attribution
and the transition cannot disagree. The pending intent and its receiver stay
as they were; they are not used.

Not run on the device yet when this patch was written.

